The Āsanas of the Rudrayāmalottaratantra (Paṭalas 23–24):
Register, Dating, and a Collation of the Translation with the Edition of Sudhākara Mālavīya
Abstract. The article examines the āsana section of the Rudrayāmalottaratantra, a Śaiva tantric work that preserves one of the largest registers of postures known from before classical haṭhayoga. It describes the composition and structure of the register (29 postures in paṭala 23 and 28 in paṭala 24), the relation between verses 23.34–35 on “thirty-two [postures], repeated in reverse order” and the actual number of descriptions, the state of research and the question of dating (with quotations from Goudriaan and Gupta and from Birch), the history of the editions, and the results of a line-by-line collation of the annotated translation with the edition of S. Mālavīya (1999) and with the critical edition of Sampurnanand Sanskrit University. It is shown that the source document reproduces Mālavīya verbatim, whereas the divergences with the critical edition are due to the corruption of the latter. A table of key readings and an account of the method of direct translation from Sanskrit are provided.
Keywords: Rudrayāmala, Uttaratantra, āsana, prāṇāyāma, kuṇḍalinī, haṭhayoga, tantric literature, textual criticism, dating, translation from Sanskrit.
1. Introduction
Śaiva tantric literature preserves dozens of registers of āsanas, yet few of them have been studied and even fewer are available in a reliable printed form. The Rudrayāmalottaratantra occupies a special place among them: its āsana section is one of the fullest registers compiled before classical haṭhayoga, and at the same time one of the least studied. It includes classical meditative postures (padmāsana, svastikāsana, baddhapadmāsana), demanding acrobatic postures (mayūrāsana, kukkuṭāsana), postures named after animals (kūrmāsana, matsyāsana, vyāghrāsana) and, finally, postures named after the social classes (brāhmaṇāsana, kṣatriyāsana, vaiśyāsana, śūdrāsana).
Crucially, the fruit of every posture in this section is not health or flexibility but mastery of vāyu: retention of the breath, the “drinking of air”, the piercing of the knots, siddhis, immortality. Here āsana is not an autonomous discipline but a stage within prāṇāyāma.
The article has three aims: (1) to describe the composition and logic of the register; (2) to present the state of the question of dating, distinguishing quotations from assessments; (3) to report the results of a collation of the annotated translation with the printed edition of S. Mālavīya (1999).
2. The Work and the Place of the Section in It
The Rudrayāmala is the most mysterious of all the yāmalas: more than fifty texts are ascribed to it, yet a “Rudrayāmala” without further qualification is nowhere to be found (Goudriaan & Gupta 1981: 47). The text usually taken to be the Rudrayāmala proper is the Uttaratantra, the “later tantra”. Its frame is a dialogue: the goddess Ānandabhairavī teaches her husband Śiva, addressing him as Mahābhairava, Nātha, Nīlakaṇṭha and Vīranātha.
Colophon of paṭala 23 (ed. Mālavīya 1999: 345):
iti śrīrudrayāmale uttaratantre mahātantrod dīpane bhāvāsananirṇaye pāśavakalpe ṣaṭcakrasārasaṅkete siddhamantraprakaraṇe bhairavībhairavasaṃvāde trayoviṃśaḥ paṭalaḥ || 23 ||
The colophon itself names the substantive headings: bhāvāsananirṇaya, “the determination of bhāva and āsana”; ṣaṭcakra-sārasaṅketa, “the summary of the six cakras”; siddhamantraprakaraṇa, “the section on the mantras of the siddhas”. The āsanas are thus embedded in a programme in which posture, breath, cakras and mantra form a single whole — which explains why the fruit of a posture is never bodily comfort but a change in the state of breath and consciousness.
The extent of the work differs between editions: 66 chapters in J. Vidyāsāgara’s edition, while the first volume of S. Mālavīya covers paṭalas 1–45. The numbering of paṭalas differs too, but the āsana material consistently falls in paṭalas 23–24.
3. Composition and Logic of the Register
The translated section contains 57 descriptions: 29 in paṭala 23 (verses 23–114) and 28 in paṭala 24 (verses 9–37). Verses 23.34–35 announce a double register: dvātriṃśad bindu-bhedāya kalpayed vāyu-vṛddhaye — “thirty-two for the piercing of the points, [and another] thirty-two for the increase of the wind”, that is, 32 postures performed in direct and reverse order (savyāpasavyayogena), giving 64.
It follows that the size of the register is neither 57 nor 64 “different” postures, but 32 positions doubled by mirror repetition. Paṭala 24 opens with a hyperbole of another order: “a hundred thousand lakhs of āsanas” on earth, created by the great ṛṣis in heaven and in the nether world (24.3). Both figures coexist: the first is technical, the second rhetorical.
The formula of a description is stable: name of the posture → its fruit → technique (position of legs, arms, head) → breath. The order of the components varies, but the set does not: a technique without a fruit is almost never found here. In paṭala 24 a conclusion is added: verses 38–39 promise longevity (cirāñjīvī) and, after one year of practice, liberation while alive (jīvanmukti).
A distinct layer is formed by the postures named after the social classes, in which the fruit is formulated socially — brahminical dignity, wealth, truthfulness, service. This differs markedly from verses 23–47, where the fruit is always yogic, and points to a late compilatory hand. The layer cannot serve to date the text, but it stands out reliably.
4. Dating
There is no precise date, and the text itself contains no chronological indications. The most definite statements belong to T. Goudriaan and S. Gupta.
On the yāmalas as a genre (Goudriaan & Gupta 1981: 40):
“The time of their origin is difficult to assess. Their formative period in any case lies before A. D. 900.”
On our tantra (Goudriaan & Gupta 1981: 47):
“The text which is most often credited with being the Rudrayāmala proper, the Uttaratantra, is certainly much later than the period in which the old Yāmala literature flourished.”
The arguments for lateness listed there (p. 47) apply in part directly to our section: the developed system of cakras; dependence on the Kulārṇava; the use of the term vāyavī for the kuṇḍalinī (in our text — vāyavī kalā in verse 25 and vāyavī-priyaḥ in Mālāsana); the enumeration of types of haṭhayoga. The same page notes that Pandey, Bagchi and Gonda likewise regard the Uttaratantra as a relatively late text (n. 63; cited via Goudriaan).
Finally, J. Birch notes the only occurrence of the term haṭhayoga in Śaiva tantras — in the fifty-fifth chapter of the Rudrayāmalottaratantra — while conceding that the passage may be an interpolation (Birch 2011, n. 90). The argument for dating stands either way: the technical use of the term is not attested before the eleventh century.
In sum: no precise date has been established, and there is no received century for the Uttaratantra. The yāmala genre took shape before 900 CE, but our text is “certainly much later”. The estimate “not earlier than the 11th–12th century”, which I adopt, is an inference from the features listed above, not a quotation: none of the authors cited names such a century.
5. Editions and the Results of the Collation
Four witnesses were used: the annotated edition of S. Mālavīya (1999: Sanskrit, interlinear Hindi commentary “Saralā”, and variant readings), the critical edition of Sampurnanand Sanskrit University, the edition of J. Vidyāsāgara (³1937, 66 chapters) and, for the extent of the work, the data on the Varendra Anusandhāna Samiti edition (1895).
A line-by-line comparison showed that the translated document reproduces Mālavīya verbatim — both the verses and the Hindi commentary. All the divergences noted earlier in the collation with the critical edition proved to be divergences not between the document and the tradition, but between Mālavīya and the critical edition; in a number of places it is the latter that is corrupt. Key readings:
