Thanks to Mālavīya’s text, passages unreadable in the critical edition become clear: “humbhaka” and “sumbhāsana” turn out to be kumbhaka and śubhāsana, “dvicīrandhra” — the eye of a needle through which the thread is passed, and “nirāśrayaḥ” — nirāvile, “in a clear, unmuddied [place]”.
The second result is textual. The verse numbering of the translation coincides with that of Mālavīya (23–114 in paṭala 23; 9–37 in paṭala 24), not with the critical edition; the earlier hypothesis of a systematic shift by one, based on OCR text, is withdrawn. In paṭala 24 a different defect was found: the announcements of the postures are displaced by half a verse, so that the name of a posture falls at the end of the preceding description. Nothing is lost in substance — only the verse boundaries, which have been restored in the translation.
6. The Fruits of Practice: A Typology
The fruits (phala) in this section fall into four levels, and this distribution is itself characteristic of the text:
Level
	Content
	Examples
	Respiratory
	mastery of vāyu, signs of kumbhaka
	six months of practice yield the marks of perfection in breath retention and “great bliss” (23.51); the “drinking of air” (vāyu-pāna) in Khañjanāsana and Sarpāsana
	Somatic
	removal of disease and bodily fault
	for the practitioner of Kārmukāsana “all enemies perish — disease and the rest” (23.86); Kūrmāsana destroys “all impurity and the sins of the kali age”
	Siddhis
	supernormal powers
	vāyavī kalā grants “great siddhi” (23.25); Granthibhedāsana “shows forth khecarī and the rest” (23.82); the power to assume any form (kāmarūpa)
	Final
	immortality and liberation
	longevity (cirāñjīvī) and, after a year of practice, jīvanmukti (24.38–39); immortality and the status of a siddha (24.5)


Such a sequence is untypical of later haṭha texts, where the emphasis shifts to health and longevity: here longevity is only the penultimate stage, and the last is liberation while alive.
7. Method of Translation
The translation was made directly from Sanskrit, without an English intermediary. Each verse is presented in six layers: Devanāgarī, IAST, Cyrillic transliteration with diacritics, word-by-word analysis, literal translation and literary translation; where Mālavīya’s Hindi commentary exists, it is translated in a separate block, and divergences from the critical edition are noted in a remark.
The refusal of an English intermediary is a matter of principle, and the collation confirmed it. The English translation of the source document occasionally adds interpretations absent from the Sanskrit: liṅga-agra is rendered “tip/base of the lingam” with the gloss “perineum”, although the text says only “the tip of the liṅga”. When translating through English, such a gloss is indistinguishable from the text; in a direct translation it remains what it is — an interpolation of the translator.
The Cyrillic transliteration follows the map supplied for the Russian version of this article (in which, for instance, -ya is rendered -йа and -ai- is rendered -аи-); quotations from English-language scholarship retain Sanskrit words in their original spelling.
8. Conclusions
1. The āsana section of the Rudrayāmalottaratantra is one of the fullest pre-haṭhayogic registers: 57 descriptions (29 in paṭala 23 and 28 in paṭala 24), conceived in verses 23.34–35 as 32 positions doubled by mirror repetition, that is, 64.
2. The verse numbering of the document coincides with Mālavīya’s edition; the document reproduces it verbatim, including the Hindi commentary.
3. In the six places examined, the critical edition of Sampurnanand Sanskrit University is corrupt, and Mālavīya offers coherent text; it is his readings that have been adopted.
4. Dating: no precise date is established. The yāmala genre took shape before 900 CE, but the Uttaratantra is “certainly much later” (Goudriaan & Gupta 1981: 40, 47); on the cumulative evidence, not earlier than the 11th–12th century (an assessment).
5. In this work āsana is not gymnastics but a stage of prāṇāyāma: the posture supports the breath, the breath pierces the knots and moves the kuṇḍalinī, and the result is described in terms of siddhi and liberation.
